      "narration": "This one line prevents more damage than everything else in this lesson combined, so let us understand why each piece is there. By default, bash carries on after a command fails. It prints the error and runs the next line anyway. Look at the first example and sit with how bad that is: the cd fails because the directory does not exist, so you are still in whatever directory you were in before, and then the rm dash r f runs there instead. That exact pattern has destroyed real production machines. Dash e stops the script at the first failure. Dash u makes an unset variable an error rather than an empty string. Remember the rm dash r f dollar DIR warning from the very first part of this course — with dash u, a misspelled variable name is caught immediately and named, instead of quietly expanding to nothing. It turns your worst class of bug into a clear error message. And pipefail fixes something genuinely surprising: normally a pipeline's exit code is only the last command's, so if the first stage fails but the last one succeeds, bash reports success. Your script thinks everything worked. With pipefail, any failing stage fails the pipeline. Put these three on line two of every script you write. There is essentially no downside, and one day it will stop a disaster.",
