      "narration": "Now the honest caveats, because dash e has a reputation for surprising people and you should know its limits rather than trusting it blindly. It deliberately does not trigger when a command's failure is being tested. Inside an if condition, inside a while, or on either side of the double ampersand and double pipe operators, a non-zero exit is a normal part of the logic, so dash e stays quiet. That is correct behaviour — you would not want grep failing to find something to kill your script when you are explicitly asking whether it found it. But it has a consequence worth knowing: if a function is called in a condition, dash e is disabled for that function's entire body, including commands deep inside it. So a function you thought was protected is not. The practical conclusion is this: treat dash e as a safety net for the failures you did not anticipate, not as your error handling strategy. Where you expect a command might fail, handle it explicitly with an if or a die. And where a failure genuinely does not matter, say so out loud by appending or-true, so that the next reader can see the decision was deliberate rather than an oversight. Explicitness costs one line and removes all ambiguity.",
