      "heading": "trap: cleaning up whatever happens",
      "narration": "Trap registers a command to run when your script receives a signal or exits, and the EXIT trap in particular is one of the most useful things in shell scripting. It fires on every exit path — a normal finish, an error under dash e, or the user pressing Control C. That guarantee is what makes it valuable. Consider the standard pattern at the top of this slide. You create a temporary directory with mktemp dash d, and immediately register a trap to delete it on exit. From that moment you can work freely, and no matter how the script ends, the temporary directory is cleaned up. Compare that with putting a delete at the bottom of the script, which does not run if the script fails in the middle, and over months you accumulate hundreds of abandoned directories filling up your disk. The habit to build is writing the trap on the very next line after creating the thing it cleans up, so the two can never drift apart. You can also trap ERR to report which line failed, and trap INT and TERM to handle interruption gracefully. The lock file example at the bottom is a genuinely useful one: it stops two copies of a cron job running at once, and because the cleanup is in a trap, a crashed run cannot leave a stale lock that blocks every future run.",
