      "narration": "When a script misbehaves, you have better options than adding echo statements everywhere. Bash dash n checks the syntax without running anything, which is worth doing before you execute something destructive. Bash dash x is the real workhorse: it prints every command as it executes, and crucially it prints them after all expansion has happened, so you see the actual values rather than the variable names. Most shell bugs are a variable containing something you did not expect, and dash x shows you that immediately. If the trace is hard to follow, set the PS4 variable to include the source file and line number, and suddenly every traced line tells you exactly where it came from. You can also turn tracing on and off around a specific section with set dash x and set plus x, which keeps the output manageable in a long script. A small but genuinely useful habit: when printing a variable for debugging, wrap it in brackets. An empty variable and one containing three spaces look identical otherwise, and that distinction is often the entire bug. And the highest-value item on this slide is the last one. ShellCheck is a static analyser for shell scripts, it is free, and it catches unquoted variables, misused test operators and dozens of other real bugs before you ship them. Run it on everything.",
